WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.020
Many of you know, I've been talking a lot
lately about youtube policies and

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.740
algorithms and how they affect our show and
other shows.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.860
There's been a lot of demonetization.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:15.329
Youtube has been bearing independent political
shows like ours, and there's a bigger conversation

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:20.640
about social media and censorship also happening,
which really exploded the day I was on the

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.650
Joe Rogan program involving Steven Crowder
and Carlos Mazda.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:30.689
And that debacle as well as recent content
and uh, policy enforcement on Twitter.

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:35.120
And this has really been relevant for years,
but lately it's been snowballing in particular

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.660
with channels like ours and other political
channels because there's a lot of misconceptions

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:46.570
about why youtube and Facebook and Twitter
are doing what they're doing as well as exactly

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.760
what it is that they're doing.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.260
So let's talk about how users and content
creators are affected, but then talk about

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.140
the big picture issues in social media and
how we should be thinking about them.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.510
I'll tell you what the problems are, but I'll
also tell you what the problems are not.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.160
And maybe most importantly, the solutions
that we should be looking at.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.660
So on platforms like Youtube and Twitter,
the more popular you are as a content creator,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:19.680
the more likely you are to be targeted censorship
and demonetization if and until you get big

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.290
enough to be exempted from some restrictions
because of a special account status that can

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.380
be available in some cases.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.260
But then social platforms will sometimes enforce
policies differently.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.080
When there's a high profile controversy, there'll
be accused of platforming, harmful speech

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.150
or of being anti-free speech.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.391
They start feeling the heat from the public,
from one side or the other, and then they

00:01:43.391 --> 00:01:48.150
start making decisions and enforcing policies
in ways we haven't necessarily seen before.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:54.940
And often those enforcements will happen in
ways that fly directly in the face of both

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.160
the letter and the spirit of the terms of
service of a platform or their mission statement

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.890
about free expression and dialogue or whatever.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:07.060
And sometimes they'll even end up making up
new policies on the fly, uh, right in the

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.860
middle of a scandal.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.220
Sometimes I'll happen to agree or disagree
with a specific call that accompany might

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.530
make, whether it's taking someone off of a
platform or reversing a ban on someone who

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.519
by didn't think actually deserved it.

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:26.939
But the problem, as I've said before, is the
inconsistency accompany will claim to stand

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:32.390
for certain values and then they'll fold under
pressure when it's a better PR move to do

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.420
the opposite and it creates a completely dysfunctional
ecosystem for everyone.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.220
And sometimes the problems are even more fundamental.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:47.370
We upload about eight videos to youtube a
day and on some days as many as 75% are demonetized

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.159
for not being quote brand safe.

00:02:50.159 --> 00:02:52.900
So we don't make any money from those videos.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.609
Sometimes it's predictable based on youtube
demonetization guidelines, but the problem

00:02:57.609 --> 00:03:04.629
is the effect that this has on our ability
to produce serious news without concerns about

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:05.629
monetization.

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:11.689
To be clear, I wouldn't and I haven't changed
the content of the show to stay monetized

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:12.689
on youtube.

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:15.079
I cover the stories that are important.

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:19.209
Youtube can and does the monetize them, but
the David Pakman show is a small business

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:23.549
and I need to make projections and have an
understanding of where will revenue come from.

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:29.680
So this application of policy is a distraction
from simply producing the best content we

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:30.680
can.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.950
If Youtube will allow us to collect revenue
from our videos, that's great.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.239
Or if a large portion of our videos will be
demonetized, then we need to deal with it

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:41.459
and figure out how else to fund the show.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:46.659
The real problem is that by all accounts,
our content should be monetized bubble.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.250
We don't show graphic violence, we don't use
profanity.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:54.150
I'm never sitting here using drugs or harassing
people or belittling people based on their

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.560
race or gender.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:03.040
Our content would be monetized were it not
for advertisers imposing their will on youtube

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.709
and youtube having little choice but to go
along with it.

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:07.719
This is not a left wing right wing thing.

00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:12.310
People on the right like to say it's happening
to them because they're conservative, but

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:17.580
it's happening to independent shows that are
across the political spectrum, including mine.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.960
It doesn't seem to be effecting the big corporate
media outlets that have youtube channels nearly

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.330
as much and sadly this is part of a theme
we've been seeing with youtube lately.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:33.090
Independent political channels on the left
and right, the David Pakman show, Kyle Kalinsky,

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.720
Steven Crowder, these types of people.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:41.170
We've realized that youtube is in general
hindering our shows and deterring people from

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:46.370
watching bolstering legacy corporate media
outlets that have youtube channels, whether

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.360
it's CNN, NBC news or late night talk shows.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.740
In July of 2018 youtube actually made an announcement
that they would start making quote authoritative

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.180
news sources more visible on the platform
in a press release.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.790
Youtube said, today we're announcing steps
we're taking with the Google news initiative

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:08.850
to support the future of news and online video
and product features.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:12.130
We've been working on to improve the news
experience on Youtube.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:17.730
We believe quality journalism requires sustainable
revenue streams and that we have a responsibility

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.670
to support innovation in products and funding
for news.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:27.180
They go on to say authoritativeness is essential
to viewers, especially during fast moving,

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.360
breaking news events.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:33.790
So we've been investing in new product features
to prominently surface authoritative videos.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.520
When you're watching a youtube video, you
see a list of other suggested videos on the

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.720
right side of the page, on desktop or below
the video on mobile.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.900
When you're finished watching a video, uh,
before the next one plays, you see a similar

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:49.460
set of suggested videos appear.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:56.190
What shows up here, these suggested videos
is controlled by youtubes recommendation algorithm.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:00.550
A large portion of our traffic has always
come from these suggestions.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.300
Someone's watching a video about whatever
youtube suggest.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:08.590
They watch another video by the David Pakman
show about a related topic.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.390
This has been a really important source of
video views for us and it's a really good

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:16.740
way of allowing people to discover our channel
for the first time.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.710
Our Youtube Dashboard tells us where our video
views come from, and we're now getting only

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:27.190
about half of the recommendations that we
were getting about a year ago and the decline

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:32.770
started right after Youtube announced they
would prioritize quote, authoritative news

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:33.940
sources.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:39.090
So not only is Youtube demonetizing the views
that we do get, they're also causing us to

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.750
get fewer views to begin with, which hurts
the channel.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:47.560
Now, this is not some underhanded plot to
destroy the David Pakman show.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:53.750
Yes, they're changing their algorithm in a
way that hurts us, but I understand what they're

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.270
trying to do and why they're trying to do
it.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:03.050
Sadly, this is the same reason that Breitbart
and info wars and other extremist fake news

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.680
sites don't show up in search results.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.440
When you go to news.google.com Google and
youtube want to play.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:16.440
Kate advertisers by preventing accusations
that they are perpetuating or allowing the

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.380
monetization of misinformation, fake news,
hate news and extremist content.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:26.370
But the reality is that whatever the motivation,
my channel, similar independent channels,

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.610
which should not be lumped in with fake news
and hate channels are getting caught up in

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.610
it.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:35.390
And frankly it could destroy many of the channels
like ours if it continues.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.210
And so far it's continuing.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:41.510
We all know the types of channels youtube
wants to make less visible.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:47.310
Uh, the Sandy Hook, conspiracy people, uh,
alt-right extremism, red pill, black bill

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:52.960
people, uh, misogynists, racist, homophobes
people targeting others on the basis of sexual

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.300
orientation.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:08:00.450
All of the types of channels that over the
years youtube has gotten a lot of flack or

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:01.550
platforming.

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:07.810
Youtube is fighting a negative image of, in
part being a garden of nut jobs and hate and

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:08.900
extremism.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.890
Youtube wants to fix their brand and maintain
their brand.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:17.200
I can see why they want to do that, but shows
like ours are getting hurt by the algorithm

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.920
to the detriment of speech and ideas to understand
how to interpret and deal with this.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:28.750
And we need to have some shared starting point,
something that many people do, which completely

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.769
fails to properly frame the conversation about
censorship and demonetization is to say that

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:38.709
youtube and other social media companies like
Twitter and Facebook are left wing.

00:08:38.709 --> 00:08:43.990
They say these platforms are silencing are
disincentivizing speech that's politically

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:49.329
incorrect or speech that is generally considered
offensive when it comes to race, nationality,

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:51.730
gender, sexual orientation, religion.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:55.970
Many people are taking this and concluding
that the social media companies have a left

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:56.970
wing agenda.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:03.399
Yes, a hateful or offensive speech is more
likely to come from someone on the right.

00:09:03.399 --> 00:09:10.519
When Steven Crowder calls Carlos Mazda, a
gay Mexican lispy queer and gets his videos

00:09:10.519 --> 00:09:13.290
the monetized, that's not something you're
really going to hear from people on the left

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:18.009
very often and it's the kind of speech that's
going to get you in trouble on some of these

00:09:18.009 --> 00:09:19.610
social media platforms.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:25.819
In that sense, it's possible that right-wingers
face more repercussions than people on the

00:09:25.819 --> 00:09:26.819
left.

00:09:26.819 --> 00:09:29.490
I don't know that to be the case, but it's
possible.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:35.829
But using that to say that these companies
are fundamentally progressive or left wing

00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:39.300
doesn't actually help us understand these
issues and it's not accurate.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:44.459
Conservatives have a conspiracy theory that
social media companies are simply bleeding

00:09:44.459 --> 00:09:48.889
heart political activists waging an ideological
war against the right.

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:54.509
The reality is these companies have no as
companies, ethical or ideological philosophies

00:09:54.509 --> 00:09:58.459
or positions, they are motivated by business
interests.

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:04.149
These companies, by the way, as I said to
Joe Rogan are also very right wing, are libertarian,

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:05.149
right?

00:10:05.149 --> 00:10:08.889
Libertarian in many ways in terms of the policies
they support and the role that they play.

00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:13.720
Google currently in an antitrust battle with
the Department of Justice, Facebook is the,

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:18.519
is the subject of a huge antitrust investigation
by the Federal Trade Commission.

00:10:18.519 --> 00:10:21.370
These companies are huge tax Dodgers.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.250
They want to self regulate.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:27.819
They lobby for deregulation the same as the
air polluters and the big banks and the gun

00:10:27.819 --> 00:10:28.999
manufacturers.

00:10:28.999 --> 00:10:35.529
What Google and Facebook and Twitter do every
day in Washington is in the most literal sense,

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:37.959
the opposite of progressivism.

00:10:37.959 --> 00:10:42.279
Having an internal culture that's socially
progressive.

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:46.680
First and foremost doesn't mean that as a
company they are progressive in their public

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.680
role.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.220
But in addition to that, even what appears
to be left wing and internal culture is often

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:54.709
just brand management.

00:10:54.709 --> 00:10:59.390
They're politically correct content policies
and the way they enforce them and what they

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:01.930
say publicly about content policies.

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:03.699
It's not virtue signaling.

00:11:03.699 --> 00:11:08.140
It's a signal to their advertising clients
and to their shareholders.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:10.189
And this should be obvious.

00:11:10.189 --> 00:11:16.790
Youtube and Twitter clearly have no authentic
principled stance on protecting offensive

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:19.999
speech or on combating offensive speech.

00:11:19.999 --> 00:11:24.439
This was most recently demonstrated by their
flip flopping, embarrassing flip flopping

00:11:24.439 --> 00:11:26.519
on the crowd or Mazda situation.

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:31.480
One side got mad at Youtube, youtube conceded
the other side got mad at Youtube.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.180
Youtube then conceded to them, they panicked.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.709
They tried to find the right balance between
protecting their image in the eyes of advertisers

00:11:37.709 --> 00:11:42.499
and uh, understanding that that's the source
of their revenue, but protecting their image

00:11:42.499 --> 00:11:43.980
in the eyes of the public as well.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:47.250
Who are the people that need to watch those
advertisements?

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.120
Youtube isn't evil or virtuous.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.749
Twitter isn't left wing or right-wing.

00:11:51.749 --> 00:11:52.869
These are corporations.

00:11:52.869 --> 00:11:56.249
They exist to generate revenue and perpetuate
themselves.

00:11:56.249 --> 00:11:59.690
So maybe you're saying, well, we still have
this problem though, that these companies

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:04.220
have a monopoly on social media and they're
silencing offensive speech.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.559
They're recklessly demonetizing political
commentators who don't deserve it.

00:12:08.559 --> 00:12:10.120
And that is what we're faced with.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:16.019
A solution that's often proposed is that we
regulate these companies that we use the government

00:12:16.019 --> 00:12:22.819
somehow to enforce free speech on the platforms,
which is already problematic language.

00:12:22.819 --> 00:12:29.110
They want us to treat these platforms like
public squares, established free speech protections

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:30.470
for all of the users.

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:32.620
But these arguments fall flat.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:37.699
The First Amendment protects you from censorship
by the government, not by private entities.

00:12:37.699 --> 00:12:43.230
The government shouldn't be able to force
a company to allow you to use their software

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.470
and their networks.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.559
This isn't a civil liberties issue.

00:12:46.559 --> 00:12:52.170
Social media companies do and should have
every legal right to maintain and enforce

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:57.740
their own content policies, to prioritize
certain content to protect their brand or

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:01.709
for any other reason that they have or none
at all, unless they're doing something like

00:13:01.709 --> 00:13:07.120
discriminating against the users of a certain
race or religion, which violates the law.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.809
What government body is going to enforce so-called
free speech on social media.

00:13:11.809 --> 00:13:15.850
Is it going to be congress whose political
bias changes every few years?

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:18.110
Will it be the FCC or the FTC?

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:23.490
Is Donald Trump is thinking of doing, whose
political bias also changes every few years.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:28.689
We know that the current administration wants
based on this, uh, executive order that they're

00:13:28.689 --> 00:13:33.839
putting together for them to be the ones who
decide based on their politics, who gets to

00:13:33.839 --> 00:13:36.939
say what and which platforms get punished.

00:13:36.939 --> 00:13:38.220
That's dangerous.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:42.449
Conservatives don't want this type of government
regulation anywhere else.

00:13:42.449 --> 00:13:47.889
They just want government regulation here
because things haven't been going their way.

00:13:47.889 --> 00:13:53.519
The free market has demonstrated that offensive
content is bad for business and they now want

00:13:53.519 --> 00:13:59.449
special, big government nanny state protections
against what is being caused by free market

00:13:59.449 --> 00:14:00.449
capitalism.

00:14:00.449 --> 00:14:05.129
Quite frankly, or the version of it that we
have, I'm being negative negatively impacted

00:14:05.129 --> 00:14:06.639
by one of these companies.

00:14:06.639 --> 00:14:08.259
Youtube has hurting me financially.

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:12.410
They're diminishing my village and my ability
to voice my opinion, but that's not making

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:17.589
me lose my objectivity about the rights of
private companies or how impractical it would

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:21.129
be to have the government oversee social media
content.

00:14:21.129 --> 00:14:26.209
The first step in the correct direction is
going to be for us to stop viewing social

00:14:26.209 --> 00:14:29.529
media as one collective thing or entity.

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:32.589
Different social media platforms serve different
purposes.

00:14:32.589 --> 00:14:35.709
Twitter has developed a service that's very
different than Facebook.

00:14:35.709 --> 00:14:37.100
The mechanics are different.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.459
The culture is different.

00:14:38.459 --> 00:14:39.660
The etiquette is different.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.100
Some platforms allow porn, some don't.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.360
Some platforms a lot of spamming, some don't.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.500
Some platforms require you use your real name
and others don't.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:52.350
All social media platforms are different because
they're geared to different markets and have

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.079
different purposes.

00:14:54.079 --> 00:14:59.870
The average person using tumbler is different
than the average person using Quora, for example,

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:04.170
or at least they use the two platforms for
very different reasons.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:09.800
Social media companies intentionally developed
their platform to have a particular environment

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:12.800
catered to the market they want to serve.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:18.720
In the same way that a platform designed for
professional networking wouldn't be any good

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.649
as a medium for sharing funny memes and vice
versa.

00:15:21.649 --> 00:15:26.579
A platform designed to serve a somewhat universal
mainstream user base and attract mainstream

00:15:26.579 --> 00:15:32.459
advertisers may not function properly as a
platform for exchanging controversial ideas

00:15:32.459 --> 00:15:33.970
or vice versa.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:39.470
Many social media platforms that are marketed
as free speech alternatives to mainstream

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:40.470
platforms.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:46.009
Some are I like personally, others seem to
be cesspools of hate and sketchiness, but

00:15:46.009 --> 00:15:53.001
the idea of forcing that content onto privately
owned platforms that don't want it and maybe

00:15:53.001 --> 00:15:56.899
financially harmed by it doesn't make sense.

00:15:56.899 --> 00:15:59.029
Youtube serves a particular purpose.

00:15:59.029 --> 00:16:04.610
It's an invaluable service to the vast number
of content creators who otherwise wouldn't

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:08.269
have a platform to so lucratively distribute
their content.

00:16:08.269 --> 00:16:11.279
It's been a really great thing for most independent
video creators.

